From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: linux-a9 <linux-a9@localhost>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 10:03:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/21] media: apple: vxd: bring up the host<->MTX mailbox (R3a)

Implements docs/periph/ds-vxd-decode-protocol.md sections 2, 3 and 6,
plus what the DEVA firmware disassembly adds to them.  Every step follows
AppleD5500's order.

Ring geometry.  0x2024 (TO_MTX) and 0x2018 (TO_HOST) are not firmware
build halves.  They are the ring geometry the firmware publishes with
the 0xa5a5a5a5 magic, as (base_bytes << 16) | capacity_words, and word
i of a ring is the register at 0x2000 + base + 4 * i.  The boot reads
them once and refuses a geometry that leaves the MMIO window, overlaps
the other ring, or cannot hold a maximum-size message.

Push (0xfffffff0062990bc).  Write the words, then 0x2030 = wr, then the
flush reads of 0x2028/0x2030/0x201c/0x2020, then the doorbell 0x88 = 1,
then a flush read of 0x2000.  The free-slot count uses Apple's formula,
rd + ~wr (+ cap), which is one word less than rounds 1-3 computed.  The
capacity comes from the geometry, not a constant.  Indices outside the
capacity, lengths of 0 or above the firmware's 0x14 words, and a ring
still full after 100 ms are errors.

Init message.  After the magic the boot sends the mandatory 0x80
message: {0x8005, firmware device address, (u8)seq << 16 | image pages,
0xffff0000, 0}.  The page count is taken from the blob size (0x17 for
the 0x17e98-byte image), not from the page-aligned allocation.  The
firmware never replies to 0x80.  Its reader publishes the new
TO_MTX_RD (0x2028) before it dispatches (fw 0x1000a9c), so the boot
waits up to 100 ms for 0x2028 to pass the message.  Only then is
hw_ready set and IRQ 157 enabled.  If the firmware is up but never takes
the message, the image and PM reference are kept so the debugfs dumps
and the ping can be used to diagnose it, and the boot returns the error.

IRQ (0xfffffff00629a0ac).  The handler reads 0x410.  If bit 20 is clear
it returns IRQ_NONE.  Otherwise it writes 0x414 = 0x100000 before it
drains, then drains TO_HOST (host rd 0x201c, fw wr 0x2020), up to the
kext's 0x70-message bound, under a spinlock.  The dispatch follows
ProcessFWMessages:
- 0xc0 completion: the fence is in w0[31:16], the status in w2; the
  fault class (0x18000000), "after core reset" and REATTEMPT_SINGLEPIPE
  are logged.
- 0xc6 contiguity warning.
- 0xc7 FW_DEVA_PANIC: fails every pending fence and turns decode off.
- 0xd1-0xd4 and 0xe1-0xe4 are dropped silently.
- Any other type is counted as unknown.
An oversized or out-of-range message resyncs the read index instead of
pinning the ring.  The firmware raises bit 20 only after it has posted a
message, so 1000 interrupts in a row with nothing to drain mean that
0x414 does not clear the level line.  The handler then disables the IRQ
with an error instead of livelocking the CPU; the hand drain (ping)
keeps working.  The rename of 0x410/0x414/0x418 from "CMD descriptor
addr/size/status" to IRQ status/clear/enable makes the known-state
epilogues read as what they are: IRQ off and all pending causes cleared.

Fences.  Eight slots, ids built as Apple does:
(submission << 4) | (packet * 2 & 0xe) | core.  The 12-bit counter
skips 0, which is the init message's fence.  A slot is armed before the
push, signalled from the drain, and failed on panic or teardown.

Ping.  `echo [0x83|0x84|0x85|0x80] > /sys/kernel/debug/apple-vxd/ping`
pushes one message, waits up to 100 ms for 0x2028 to pass it, drains
TO_HOST by hand (Apple's timeout path calls ProcessInterrupt manually),
then gives a fence completion 20 ms.  The DEVA dispatcher sends
0x83-0x85 straight back to its reader loop (jump table at fw 0x1000ad4),
so "consumed, no completion" is the expected answer.  0x80 re-sends the
init message, as Apple's recovery path does.  An empty write sends 0x83.
Every step is a flushed STEP line, and the shell sees the errno.

Debugfs mbox now decodes the geometry, both index pairs with the free
count, the consumed word (0x0), the firmware PC (0x200c), the message
counter (0x2010), IRQ status/enable, the state flags, the counters,
the live fences, and the first 64 words of each ring.

A protocol self-test runs at probe, before debugfs exists, and touches
no MMIO.  It checks the free-slot formula, ring wrap, the consumption
check, header packing, the init message and the GART entry arithmetic.
If it fails, the firmware boot is refused.

Decode jobs still fail: apple_vxd_submit() now says the mailbox is up
and the command-stream generator (R3c) is missing.

Signed-off-by: linux-a9 <linux-a9@localhost>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <maintainer@example.invalid>
